{"actor_ref":"claude","class":"draft_history","document_uuid":"01a1a54a-324a-76bd-8416-88b1212f29a4","id":"01a1a644-4198-77bb-9290-4087b5d3ce7d","idempotency_key":"7f690d38b90b330d2e99592eae4d07eefa59d35811de9cc9782b45b2593b994f","occurred_at":"2026-11-04T11:14:13Z","payload":{"note":"The MCP server exists. crates/ai/src/mcp.rs is the protocol as a pure function - initialize, tools/list, tools/call, ping and the two notifications - so it is testable without a socket: 13 tests in crates/ai/tests/mcp.rs cover the envelope, that a notification is never answered, that tools/list carries the same schemas the app uses (and does not leak internal effect metadata onto the wire), that a call runs the same host, and that an unknown tool, bad arguments, an unknown method, a non-object request and a missing method each give the right JSON-RPC code rather than a panic. Both transports use ONE registry, host and approval queue, which is the design's 'one registry, one queue, one audit log'. App side: POST /mcp with a bearer token (401 before dispatch), a per-connection rate limit of 240 requests a minute (429; measured live as 240 x 200 then 10 x 429), a 1 MiB ceiling, GET /api/mcp/config and POST /api/mcp/rotate (rotating leaves the old token 401 and the new one 200), and 'rusty-brick-chess mcp' for stdio, which inherits process trust and needs no token (verified by piping initialize and tools/list through it). The token lives in settings under mcp_token and is generated on first use; comparison accumulates differences over the whole string, so a near miss is not a timing signal. Found along the way: the app crate sits outside the workspace, so 'make check' has never linted it, and it carries a backlog of lints - three are fixed here, the rest are a separate pass rather than something to sweep in silently.","target":"t-745d"},"type":"work.note_added","v":2}
